WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.420 --> 00:00:04.260
(wailing in pain) "I'm sorry!"

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:07.380
So, in the very first sentence of the

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:09.440
New York Times' review of Reservoir Dogs,

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:11.200
Critic Vincent Canby wrote

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.320
"it's been an unusually good year for the discovery

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:14.940
of first rate, new American

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.120
film directors'',

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:17.720
Then goes on to list four films that

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:19.260
you've never seen by four directors

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:21.020
that you've never heard of,

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:22.060
none of whom lasted more than a decade

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:24.040
making movies, before continuing,

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:26.900
now add to that list the name of Quentin Tarantino.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:28.920
I guess one out of five ain't bad.

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:29.820
(Screaming)

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:33.780
"You're gonna be okay!"

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:36.360
25 years after that review, Tarantino

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:37.800
is probably the most popular

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:40.300
living, working director in the world,

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.080
after maybe Spielberg and Scorsese.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.040
His eight films have grossed over

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:47.340
$1.5 billion worldwide, and writing about

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:49.560
his work could fill a small library.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:55.440
The quarter century milestone is a

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:56.660
good opportunity to look back at

00:00:56.660 --> 00:00:58.960
how Tarantino's first film has aged.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:01.160
I've a slight obsession with firsts,

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:02.840
with how things begin, and

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:04.540
the first shot of Reservoir Dogs is

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:06.540
probably the first that I think about

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:08.040
the most. Okay, yeah, obviously

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:09.540
I know this is not technically the

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:11.000
first shot of the movie, but

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:11.820
I've always sort of viewed the

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:13.500
diner scene that precedes the

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.500
opening credits as a kind of overture

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:17.200
of Tarantino's entire filmography.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:19.660
Traditionally overtures are mood setters.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:21.200
A little bit of music that sets the

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:22.860
tone for what's about to follow.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.220
It's only natural that in Tarantinoland,

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:27.140
the overture is a seven-minute conversation

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:29.640
about Madonna songs and the merits of tipping.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.360
''But no, society says 'don't tip these

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:33.240
guys over here, but tip these guys over here',

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:34.060
that's bullshit."

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:36.380
So, the first shot of the story

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:38.300
proper is this, and it's one of the most

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:40.300
electrifying openings in film history.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.080
Like the first notes in the first

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:43.300
Led Zeppelin album.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:45.080
Before you have a chance to blink,

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:47.040
you're involved. Your mind fans

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:48.520
forward and backward through time.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:50.400
It's usual in film for actions to have consequences,

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.920
but here, consequences have actions;

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:54.940
actions you begin to imagine.

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:55.700
A heist gone wrong.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:02:00.760
[Led Zeppelin's "Good Times Bad Times" playing in the background]

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:03.060
And just like you're in the past,

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:04.600
you're also in the future,

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:06.160
calculating how long this guy

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:07.260
has left to live and what can be

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:08.340
done to save him.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:08.920
And of course,

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:10.780
like Mr. Orange bleeding out,

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:12.980
you can't escape the present either,

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:14.860
because that's what pain is;

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:16.980
a brutal and specific attention

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:17.960
to the present moment.

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:20.100
It's that pain, that panicked feeling

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:21.500
of time running out, that

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:23.380
separates this film from others

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:24.860
that begin in media res.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:26.820
Whenever I create anything,

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:28.520
I always hold it up against the opening

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:30.800
of Reservoir Dogs; is the beginning as

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:32.740
attention capturing as this?

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:34.420
Does it have the same kinetic energy

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.260
launching you into the narrative?

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:37.480
It's here too, of course, that we're

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:39.100
introduced to the technique that made

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:41.720
Tarantino famous: the non-linear structure

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:42.720
of his story-telling.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:44.820
In interviews, Tarantino compared the

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:46.660
Byzantine tracks of his early films

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:47.980
to novel-writing.

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:49.780
"Novels have always had just a

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:51.780
complete freedom to pretty much

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:53.180
tell their story any way they saw fit,

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:55.260
alright, and that's kinda what I'm-

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.260
That's kinda what I'm trying to do."

00:02:57.260 --> 00:02:58.760
But while I think the comparison to

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:00.540
fiction is apt, I also think that there

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:01.800
is a relationship here again,

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:03.180
with music.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:04.380
One of the things that I think helped

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:06.360
Reservoir Dogs age so well is

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:08.380
Tarantino's instinctive sense for

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:09.420
narrative momentum.

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:11.040
To me, the ordering of the movie

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:13.260
is a lot like an underexamined aspect of

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:15.840
popular music; the sequencing of albums.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:17.700
One of the reasons I love Abbey Road,

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:19.120
for example, one of the reasons

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:20.460
I've listened to it so many times

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:21.900
all the way through, is because the

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:23.620
song sequence carries me through.

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:24.960
Even in the first half, where there's

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:27.120
no medley, the arrangement has thrust.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:28.660
I mean, just imagine the change

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:31.160
in feeling if the album began with, say,

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:32.740
Maxwell's Silver Hammer.

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:36.720
[Beatle's "Maxwell's silver Hammer, playing in the background]

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:39.680
Now imagine the change in feeling if

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:41.780
Reservoir Dogs began here.

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:48.780
"Say hello to a motherfucker who's inside.

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:50.560
Cabot's doing a job and take a big

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:51.740
fat guess who he wants on the team?"

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:53.280
The sequencing in Reservoir Dogs

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:54.900
has the same natural rhythm as an

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:55.780
album cycle does;

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:57.600
offering a variety in tempo,

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:00.460
from intense, to reflective, and back,

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:03.240
while linking corresponding scenes together.

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:05.480
All Tarantino's films are constructed this way,

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:07.320
like albums; more like albums,

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:08.780
I think, than novels.

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:09.820
Even the ones with more

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:10.720
straightforward narratives.

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:16.100
[Rick Ross' "100 Black Coffins", playing in the background]

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:18.280
"And part of my thing when I'm

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:19.400
coming up with an idea

00:04:19.580 --> 00:04:21.920
is to go through that record room

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:23.300
and go through those records,

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:25.080
and to kinda 'find the music'

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:27.960
or the 'personality' of a given movie.

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:29.680
It's like I'm looking for the rhythm

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:31.680
that this movie needs to play in,

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:33.480
I'm looking for the spirit and the rhythm

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:34.880
that this movie needs to play in."

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:36.240
The other thing that Tarantino is

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:38.140
most known for is his dialogue,

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:39.380
and of course, some of the references

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:41.620
in Reservoir Dogs haven't aged so well.

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:43.140
"You know who she looked like?

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:45.380
She looked like, uh, Christie Love.

00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:46.340
Remember that TV Show?"

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:49.440
"Get Christie Looooove"

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:53.720
"Get Christie Looooove!"

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:54.780
"She's a cop?" "Oh yeah!"

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:56.040
"You're under arrest, sugar!"

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:56.540
(laughing)

00:04:57.140 --> 00:04:59.300
Nobody today remembers the show

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:00.100
Get Christie Love,

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:02.460
but what's more important here is the concept;

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:04.320
people still talk about TV shows,

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:06.180
entertainment is still a common language

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:07.360
between strangers.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:10.160
"Taking genre characters in genre situations

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:13.580
allright, and giving them a real life spin,

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:15.580
have them sound like real people,

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:17.300
like, y'know, me and my friends,

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:18.440
and just other people,

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:20.180
and make references that other people make..."

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:22.400
Reservoir Dogs translates into 2017

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:24.300
because the humanity and the comedy

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:26.700
that Tarantino breathed into genre films

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:28.940
is still felt all over Hollywood.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:30.060
"Footloose;

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:32.820
and in it, a great hero

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:35.840
named Kevin Bacon

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:38.680
teaches an entire city full of people

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:40.100
with sticks up their butts that

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:42.040
dancing is the greatest thing there is."

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:44.780
I think the only context in which

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:46.680
Reservoir Dogs hasn't aged so well

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:49.080
is within Tarantino's own filmography.

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:51.760
For all the purchase it has on film culture

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:53.500
and popular culture, it's still very clearly

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:55.300
his most amateur movie.

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:57.420
It's impossible to say how it would

00:05:57.420 --> 00:05:59.000
be received today if it was released,

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:00.100
because so much of cinema

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:02.140
in the past 25 years is influenced by

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:03.100
this style.

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:04.880
But I think Siskel and Ebert

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:07.380
got pretty close in their original reviews.

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:09.280
"I liked the movie as far as it went,

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:10.920
I wanted it to go further and try more."

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:12.860
"I had the same reaction, that it was

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:15.400
a lot of- an exercise in style, uhh

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:17.200
but then I got that really quick,

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:19.200
I mean, I understand what he's trying to do here,

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:22.840
which is to show things in crime movies

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:25.140
that crime movies don't show.

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:28.080
For example, the sloppiness, the humour...

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:31.420
But we get that within 15 minutes"

00:06:31.420 --> 00:06:33.200
The missing piece to Reservoir Dogs

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:35.800
is the deeper character studies and exploration

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:38.720
of big ideas that you get in Pulp Fiction,

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:41.440
in Jackie Brown, Inglourious Basterds, and

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:42.280
Django Unchained.

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:44.460
However, I do think that there is one

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:45.940
point in Reservoir Dogs

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:49.000
where Tarantino does reach for something deeper.

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:51.100
The commode story sequence.

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:53.800
All of Tarantino's work comments on

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:55.740
the nature of storytelling itself,

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:57.520
but the part when Mr. Orange,

00:06:57.520 --> 00:06:58.340
an undercover cop,

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:00.560
learns a fake story to sell the criminals

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:02.000
on his credibility,

00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:04.080
is one of the best instances of this

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:05.060
in all his movies.

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:06.900
He splits the sequence into four parts:

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:08.620
Orange learning the scene,

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:10.080
Orange rehearsing the scene,

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:12.360
Orange performing the story for an audience,

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:14.940
and finally, the story coming alive.

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:17.340
It's an elegant illustration of how words

00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:19.400
can transform into stories,

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:21.400
and stories into reality,

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:23.440
or into movies.

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:24.700
And it's a nice touch that the only thing

00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:27.020
that keeps the cops from noticing the drugs

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:29.220
is a similar storytelling situation,

00:07:29.220 --> 00:07:32.700
because stories can keep us from seeing reality too.

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:37.020
"Buddy, I'm gonna shoot you in the face

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:39.620
if you don't put your hands on the fucking dash!"

00:07:39.620 --> 00:07:41.760
As an exercise in genre and style,

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:43.800
Reservoir Dogs is superb,

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:45.380
one of the best in film, and

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:47.940
if it falls just short of the transcendent

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:49.840
heights that Tarantino would later go on to reach,

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:53.440
that's not really a criticism because,

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:55.200
I mean, let's see you reach try to reach

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:56.340
''just short of transcendent"

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:58.000
with your first movie.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:07:59.280
I think a good rule of thumb

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:02.140
for how well films have aged is

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:04.080
simply whether or not we keep

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:04.800
watching them.

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:08.740
25 years on, I'm still watching Reservoir Dogs,

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:11.180
and I'll bet that'll still be true

00:08:11.480 --> 00:08:14.080
25 years and more from now.

